# 15. Test the complete business flow

We can temporarily expose `order-service` using port-forward:

```bash
kubectl port-forward service/order-service 3003:3000
```

Now from your local machine:

```bash
curl -X POST http://localhost:3003/orders \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"userId":"1","productId":"101","quantity":2}'
```

The request flow is:

```text
Your Laptop
     │
     │ POST /orders
     ▼
order-service
     │
     ├──────────────► user-service
     │                    │
     │                    ▼
     │                 User Pod
     │
     └──────────────► product-service
                          │
                          ▼
                       Product Pod
```

And the response comes back through `order-service`.

---

# 16. Why Service is important

Suppose:

```text
user-service
```

has:

```text
Pod A = 10.81.1.10
Pod B = 10.81.1.11
```

Tomorrow Pod A dies.

Kubernetes creates:

```text
Pod C = 10.81.1.20
```

Your `order-service` doesn't care.

It still calls:

```text
http://user-service:3000
```

The Service finds the healthy Pods.

That's one of the fundamental reasons Kubernetes is useful for microservices.

